WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.620
Retries, redeliveries, and double clicks are part of real-world software.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.270
But receiving the same request again shouldn’t mean doing the same work twice.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.490
Namastack Once brings transactional idempotency to Spring Boot,

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:15.780
using the database you already have.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:20.660
It commits your business writes and a duplicate-detection marker in one transaction,

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.970
so repeated operations with the same scope and key can be safely skipped.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.440
If an operation fails, that transaction rolls back,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.780
allowing a later delivery to try again.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.690
With a simple API, flexible retention, and optional metrics and tracing,

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:39.070
it fits naturally into your Java or Kotlin application.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.510
With Namastack Once, you can handle repeated requests with confidence

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.530
and focus on building what comes next.
